From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: marty <marty@fyai.co.nz>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 16:13:40 +1200
Subject: [PATCH] RetroPlayer: wire up hardware rendering

The machinery landed in the previous commits; this connects it.

- IRenderBufferPool gains EnableHardwareRendering(depth, stencil), alongside
  the DestroyContext() hook that is already there. Pools that cannot serve a
  hardware client ignore it and keep answering IsCompatible() as before, so the
  sysmem path is untouched.
- CRPRenderManager::Create() activates the pools and reports whether any of
  them took the job. No GL work happens here: the FBO pool creates its context
  and framebuffer lazily in CreateRenderBuffer(), which runs on the game loop
  thread, where the client's context has to be current anyway. That is what
  makes the old "//! @todo This must be called from the rendering thread"
  moot rather than ignored.
- CRPRenderManager::RenderFrame() publishes the buffer the client rendered
  into, mirroring the submitted-buffer branch of AddFrame(). There is no CPU
  access to close here - a framebuffer was lent, not memory.
- CRetroPlayerRendering::OpenStream() passes the client's real depth and
  stencil requirements through and calls Create(); CloseStream() flushes, and
  leaves the pools' GL resources to EndEvent(), which runs on the thread that
  owns them.
- CGameClientStreams::EnableHardwareRendering() stops returning false.

Unproven: none of this can execute until the box runs the image. It is
reachable only by a client that calls SET_HW_RENDER, and no such client is
installed there with content to match, so the worst case is that hardware
rendering still does not work - not that anything which works today stops.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
